\section{Choose an Image Test That Distinguishes Two Operation Orders}

A learner may pass a picture exercise with an implementation that applies two image transformations in the wrong order if all the practice inputs are symmetric. An illustrative teaching tool can search a small catalog for an example on which the two candidate implementations disagree. It receives two pure, total transformations and a finite, ordered collection of small images. Image equality means equal dimensions and equal colors at each coordinate; source labels or rendering caches are not part of the comparison. The result is a concrete question to pose to the learner, or a report that the catalog did not separate the candidates.

\begin{verbatim}
contrast(images, first, second):
  for img in images in catalog order:
    # Show the intermediate picture for either prediction.
    a = first(img); b = second(img)
    expected = second(a); reversed = first(b)
    if not equal_pixels(expected, reversed):
      # Delay the answer until after the prediction.
      prompt = ask_for_ordered_result(img, first, second)
      return Lesson(prompt, a, b, expected, reversed)
  return NoWitnessInCatalog
\end{verbatim}

Use rows of colored squares, one red square followed by one blue square in the first input. Let \texttt{first} reverse the row and \texttt{second} append green on the right. Learners predicting reverse followed by append should see \verb|[blue, red]| and then \verb|[blue, red, green]|. The other order shows \verb|[red, blue, green]| and then \verb|[green, blue, red]|. If a learner predicts the second final row, the tool can ask which intermediate row they expected, rather than immediately treating the mistake as a misunderstanding of reversal. Both outputs have the same color histogram, so equality based only on counts would find no witness.

The invariant is that every emitted lesson uses a single input under the same two primitive rules; the only difference is their order. Searching a finite catalog makes the test cheap and repeatable, but \texttt{NoWitnessInCatalog} is not proof that two operations commute. A useful follow-up asks learners to create a different distinguishing input or explain why a chosen pair of operations gives the same result in either order.
